<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">ailments</ansi>

Three sicknesses can lay a member of your company low. Each has one cause
you can see coming, one clear penalty in battle, and a remedy you make at
camp from herbs you gathered. They are never random and never come from a
place: they come from what your company chose to do.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Ailment</ansi>    <ansi fg="yellow">Caught by</ansi>                          <ansi fg="yellow">In battle</ansi>         <ansi fg="yellow">Lasts</ansi>
  Chill       walking or ending a journey out     -10% damage dealt  4 battles
              of doors, or ending a camp rest,
              while Frozen
  Gut-ache    eating raw game meat                +10% damage taken  3 battles
  Fever       a lasting puncture wound left       -15% damage dealt  5 battles
              open through 3 battles

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where they come from</ansi>

  Chill      Frozen means frostbitten or worse (<ansi fg="command">help temperature</ansi>): a member whose
             warmth reads frostbitten, hypothermic or freezing to death. A
             comfortable or merely chilled member is fine, and a step or a
             journey's end under a roof, or a night at an inn, never gives one. Warm
             clothes, a fire and a warming draught keep it away.
  Gut-ache   <ansi fg="command">eat raw game meat</ansi> (or <ansi fg="command">eat raw game meat [member]</ansi>) fills 10 Hunger and
             always gives it. <ansi fg="command">company eat</ansi> never serves raw meat: cook it first
             (<ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>).
  Fever      each battle a member fights with a lasting puncture wound still
             open counts one (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>); on the third the wound festers.
             Treating the wound (a bandage, a surgeon's kit, tending, an inn or
             a physician) starts its count over, even if it stays partly
             open. A member with a fever is not counted again.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What they do</ansi>

An ailment sets the battle condition like hunger does (<ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>): it is
fixed when the battle begins, adds to any hunger or thirst penalty, and
counts one battle off for each battle the member fights. When the battles
run out it fades by itself. A well-kept company with no ailments fights as
it always has.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Remedies</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp prepare remedy</ansi> [member|all]
  At your camp, makes the remedy for each ailing member from the herbs the
  company carries, and ends the ailment at once. It asks for all the herbs
  first and uses none if any are short.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Ailment</ansi>    <ansi fg="yellow">Remedy</ansi>                     <ansi fg="yellow">Herbs</ansi>
  Chill       thyme tea                  2 wild thyme
  Gut-ache    thyme and mushroom tisane  1 wild thyme, 1 mushroom
  Fever       cooling fever draught      1 glacial mint, 1 wild thyme

Thyme tea is common knowledge. You make the other two only once you have
worked them out: <ansi fg="command">camp prepare remedy with</ansi> [herb] [herb] tries exactly that mix of
herbs on your ailing company. The right mix for someone who is ill cures them
and goes into your recipe book (<ansi fg="command">help recipes</ansi>), so you never name it again. Any
other mix, or the right one when nobody needs it, only uses up its herbs. A
plain <ansi fg="command">camp prepare remedy</ansi> makes only the remedies you know.

Wild thyme and mushrooms grow in most places, and glacial mint most often on
the Frost Lake, and now and then as a rare find elsewhere (<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi>).
A remedy is used up as it is made: you cannot carry one, and nobody buys one. <ansi fg="command">camp supplies</ansi> lists the herbs each remedy you know takes and
how many you carry. The rules are those of <ansi fg="command">help camp supplies</ansi>: an established
camp in the room, no rest running and no fight.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where you see them</ansi>

The fight opens with a "Going in:" line naming each member's ailments;
<ansi fg="command">status</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">conditions</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">survival</ansi> list them with the battles left; and
in the web client the vitals strip warns of each ailing member, Company >
Status shows the "In battle" line, the Camp tab lists who is ailing while
you are at your camp, with a Make remedies button, and the battle screen marks the member.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help temperature</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help camp supplies</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi>.
